# see it work

every page here is one file in one folder, rendered to six surfaces by the server it describes. you are reading one of those renderings now. fetch another and compare: same words, same source file.

## this site, on each network

=> gemini://unseenservant.dev/ gemini: gemini://unseenservant.dev/
=> gopher://unseenservant.dev:1024/ gopher: gopher://unseenservant.dev:1024/
=> spartan://unseenservant.dev:3300/ spartan: spartan://unseenservant.dev:3300/
=> nex://unseenservant.dev:1900/ nex: nex://unseenservant.dev:1900/
=> https://unseenservant.dev/ web: https://unseenservant.dev/

finger is the odd one out: it answers with a short generated profile, not the content tree. the finger(1) command cannot ask on any port but 79, so use a client that can, or netcat:

```two ways to finger this host
lagrange:  finger://unseenservant.dev:7979
terminal:  nc unseenservant.dev 7979
```

## the odd ports

gopher's canonical port is 70, spartan's is 300, finger's is 79. all three are privileged, and usv runs without privileges, so this host serves them higher up. a bare gopher://unseenservant.dev/ sits there and times out; with :1024 it answers.

=> addresses.gmi every address and port, with the reasons
=> /usv the same list, generated live from the running configuration

## what you just saw

no build step ran and no copies were synchronised. the folder renders once, on save: to gemtext as it stands, to gopher menus, to a finger profile, and to plain classless html. the render takes under two seconds, so a page is live everywhere about as fast as it can be saved.

the rendered html tree is also a complete static site on its own. copy it anywhere, an onion mirror, a usb stick, a cdn, and it works with no server behind it.

## two minutes, kept

if this page reached you two ways, the claim on the front page is demonstrated, not asserted. the rest of the site keeps the same rule: no claim without something that verifies it.

=> decide.gmi the evidence and the risks
=> run.gmi a capsule of your own, ten minutes
=> index.gmi back to the plate
